    56	\begin{document}
    57	
    58	\maketitle
    59	
    60	\begin{abstract}
    61	  This note describes a SAT encoding for the $n$-fractions puzzle
    62	  which is problem \texttt{041} of the CSPLib. Using a
    63	  SAT solver we obtain a solution for two of the six remaining open
    64	  instances of this problem.
    65	\end{abstract}
    66	
    67	
    68	\section{Introduction}
    69	
    70	The $n$-fractions puzzle \cite{csplib:prob041} is problem \texttt{041} of
    71	the CSPLib. The original puzzle is specified as follows: find
    72	nine distinct non-zero digits, $\{A,B,C,D,E,F,G,H,I\}$, that satisfy
    73	\[ 
    74	  \frac{A}{BC} + \frac{D}{EF} + \frac{G}{HI}  = 1
    75	\]
    76	where $BC$ is shorthand for $10B +C$, $EF$ for $10E +F$, and
    77	$H$ for $10H + I$.
    78	%
    79	A simple generalization is as follows: find $3n$ nonzero
    80	digits, $x_i,y_i,z_i$ ($1\leq i\leq n$), satisfying
    81	\begin{equation}\label{eq:problem_specification}
    82	    \sum_{i=1}^n \frac{x_i}{y_iz_i} = 1
    83	  \end{equation}
    84	%
    85	where $y_iz_i$ is shorthand for $10y_i + z_i$ and the number of
    86	occurrences of each digit in $\{1,\ldots,9\}$ is between 1 and
    87	$\lceil n/3\rceil$. 
    88	%
    89	An interesting problem is to find the greatest $n$ such that at
    90	least one solution exists.
    91	%
    92	Since each fraction is at least $1/99$, this family of problems has
    93	solutions for at most $n\leq 99$. Malapert and Provillard prove in a
    94	recent paper~\cite{malapert2017puzzle} that the puzzle has no solution for $n\geq
    95	45$. 
    96	
    97	Two models are described in the literature~(see \cite{malapert2017puzzle}) to solve the
    98	$n$-fractions puzzle. 
    99	%
   100	The \emph{division model} handles
   101	Equation~\eqref{eq:problem_specification} with floating point
   102	arithmetic. This approach returns invalid solutions because of
   103	rounding errors. 
   104	%
   105	The \emph{product model} only needs integer arithmetic because
   106	Equation~\eqref{eq:problem_specification} is reformulated as follows:
   107	\begin{equation}
   108	  \label{eq:product_model}
   109	  \sum_{i=1}^n \left(x_i\prod_{k\neq i} y_kz_k\right) = \prod_{i=1}^n y_kz_k
   110	\end{equation}
   111	%
   112	The main problem with the product model is that the number of bits
   113	required to represent the products grows exponentially with the size
   114	of $n$. For example, the multiplication term on the right side of
   115	Equation~\eqref{eq:product_model} overflows a 32-bit integer for
   116	$n=6$.
   117	
   118	Malapert and Provillard~\cite{malapert2017puzzle} propose an \emph{integer
   119	  factorization model} and demonstrate that applying this model they
   120	can find solutions for all of the instances with $n<45$ except for
   121	six: where $n\in\{36,39,41,42,43,44\}$.
   122	%
   123	Their approach comprises two basic ideas:
   124	%
   125	The first idea is to solve the following constraint instead of
   126	  that expressed as Equation~\eqref{eq:problem_specification}:
   127	\begin{equation}
   128	  \label{eq:specification_with_lcm}
   129	  \sum_{i=1}^n x_i\times \frac{L}{y_iz_i} = L
   130	\end{equation}
   131	%
   132	where $L$ is the lowest common multiple of the integers
   133	$\sset{y_iz_i}{1\leq i\leq n}$. In this formalization, each of the
   134	terms, $\frac{L}{y_iz_i}$ on the left side of
   135	Equation~\eqref{eq:specification_with_lcm} is an integer. In theory,
   136	the products in Equation~\eqref{eq:specification_with_lcm} still grow
   137	exponentially. In practice, based on this formulation, it is possible
   138	to solve large $n$-fractions puzzles.
   139	%
   140	The second idea is to represent the integer variables in
   141	  Equation~\eqref{eq:specification_with_lcm} in terms of their prime
   142	  factorizations.
   143	
   144	
   145	
   146	In this note we describe a simple \emph{LCM model} for the
   147	$n$-fractions problem. The approach is based on
   148	Equation~\eqref{eq:specification_with_lcm}. We encode the constraints
   149	of this model to SAT using a standard binary representation for
   150	integers. Our approach is able to solve two of the instances left open
   151	in the paper by Malapert and Provillard~\cite{malapert2017puzzle}. These are the
   152	36-fraction puzzle and the 39-fraction puzzle.
   153	
   154	
   155	\section{The LCM Constraint Model}
   156	
   157	In this section we describe a simple \emph{LCM model} for the
   158	$n$-fractions problem in terms of finite integer constraints.  These
   159	are then compiled to CNF using the finite-domain constraint compiler
   160	\bee~\cite{Metodijair2013} which compiles constraints to CNF. The
   161	(conjunctions of) constraints in our model (in \bee\ syntax) are
   162	detailed below as framed text.
   163	
   164	\subsection{Domain and Counting Constraints}
   165	\label{section:domain}
   166	For $1\leq i\leq n$, the variables $x_i,y_i,z_i$ take integer values
   167	in the domain $\{1,\ldots,9\}$. The number of occurrences of each
   168	digit is constrained to be between 1 and $\lceil{n/3}\rceil$.
   169	%
   170	The variables $y_iz_i = 10\times y_i+z_i$ take integer values in the
   171	domain $\{11,\ldots,99\}$. 
   172	
   173	In \bee\ an integer variable $\mathtt{x}$ is declared to be in unary
   174	or binary representation, $\mathtt{new\_int(x,lb,ub)}$ or
   175	$\mathtt{new\_binary(x,lb,ub)}$, where $\mathtt{lb}$ and $\mathtt{ub}$
   176	are lower and upper bounds. 
   177	
   178	The variables $x_i,y_i,z_i$ and $y_iz_i$ are represented in unary
   179	representation.  The variables $x_i$ and $y_iz_i$ are represented also
   180	through channelling to their binary representation. This is because
   181	the counting constraints (on the digits) are best encoded to CNF using
   182	the unary representation while the arithmetic constraints described in
   183	Sections~\ref{section:lcm_constraints}
   184	and~\ref{section:puzzle_constraints} are best encoded to CNF using the
   185	binary representation.
   186	%
   187	In the constraint model, detailed as Figure~\ref{modelpart1}, we denote
   188	the digits $[x_1,\ldots,x_n, y_1,\ldots,y_n, z_1,\ldots,z_n,]$ by
   189	$[dig_1,\ldots,dig_{3n}]$ and then the (Boolean) variables $dig_{i,j}$
   190	denote that $dig_i$ takes value $j$ and the (integer) variables $s_j$
   191	denote the number of occurrences of the value $j$ among
   192	$[dig_1,\ldots,dig_{3n}]$ (for $1\leq i\leq 3n, 1\leq j\leq 9$).
   193	%
   194	
   195	\begin{figure}[t]
   196	  \centering
   197	  \fbox{\[ \begin{array}{l} 
   198	  \displaystyle\bigwedge_{i=1}^n 
   199	  \set{new\_int(x_i,1,9), new\_int(y_i,1,9), new\_int(z_i,1,9), new\_int(y_iz_i,11,99), \\
   200	       channel\_int2binary(x_i), channel\_int2binary(y_iz_i),\\
   201	       int\_array\_lin\_eq([10,1],[y_i,z_i],y_iz_i)
   202	      } \\
   203	   \displaystyle\bigwedge_{i=1}^{3n} \bigwedge_{j=1}^9  
   204	   \set{int\_eq\_reif(dig_i,j,dig_{i,j})
   205	       } \\
   206	   \displaystyle\bigwedge_{j=1}^9 
   207	   \set{new\_int(s_j,1,\lceil n/3\rceil),
   208	        bool\_array\_sum\_eq([dig_{1,j},\ldots dig_{3n,j}],s_j)
   209	       }
   210	   \end{array}
   211	\]}
   212	  
   213	  \caption{The \bee\ model: part~1.}\label{modelpart1}
   214	\end{figure}
   215	
   216	
   217	
   218	
   219	
   220	\subsection{Symmetry Breaking and Redundant Constraints}
   221	\label{section:symmetry}
   222	
   223	We add the symmetry breaking constraints and a redundant constraint
   224	proposed by Frisch~\cite{frisch2004symmetry}
   225	\begin{equation}\label{eq:symmetry}
   226	  (y_i,z_i,x_i)\leq_{lex}(y_{i+1},z_{i+1},x_{i+1})~~ 1\leq i<n
   227	\end{equation}
   228	%
   229	\begin{equation}
   230	  \min_{1\leq i\leq n}y_iz_i \leq \sum_{i=1}^nx_i\leq \max_{1\leq i\leq n}y_iz_i
   231	\end{equation}
   232	For the \bee\ syntax see Figure~\ref{modelpart2}.
   233	
   234	\begin{figure}[b]
   235	  \centering
   236	  \fbox{\[ \begin{array}{l} 
   237	    \displaystyle\bigwedge_{i=1}^{n-1}
   238	    \set{int\_arrays\_lex([y_i,z_i,x_i],[y_{i+1},z_{i+1},x_{i+1}])
   239	        } \\
   240	    \bigwedge\set{new\_int(r,n,9n),  int\_array\_sum\_eq([x_1,\ldots,x_9],r), \\
   241	               new\_int(min,11,99), int\_array\_min([y_1z_1,\ldots y_nz_n],min),\\
   242	               new\_int(max,11,99), int\_array\_max([y_1z_1,\ldots y_nz_n],max),\\
   243	               int\_leq(min,r),   int\_leq(r,max)
   244	        }
   245	      \end{array}
   246	  \] }
   247	  
   248	  \caption{The \bee\ model part 2.}\label{modelpart2}
   249	  
   250	\end{figure}
   251	
   252	\subsection{LCM Constraints}
   253	\label{section:lcm_constraints}
   254	
   255	The least common multiple, $L$ of a set of positive integers $S$ is
   256	the smallest positive integer that is divisible by each of the
   257	integers in $S$. In the context of
   258	Equation~\eqref{eq:specification_with_lcm}, it is sufficient if $L$ is
   259	any common multiple.
   260	
   261	We introduce integer variables $L$ and $\{d_1,\ldots,d_n\}$. The
   262	variable $L$ takes values in the domain $\{1,\ldots \mathit{maxL}\}$
   263	where $\mathit{maxL}$ is a parameter of the encoding. The variables
   264	$d_i$ take values in the domain $\lceil \mathit{maxL}/11 \rceil$.  The
   265	following constraint states that $L$ is divided by each of the
   266	numbers $y_iz_i$. This constraint also ``determines'' the variables
   267	$d_i$, or more precisely, the relation between the variables
   268	$y_iz_i,L$ and $d_i$.
   269	\begin{equation}
   270	  \label{eq:lcm}
   271	  \bigwedge_{i=1}^n y_iz_i \times d_i = L
   272	\end{equation}
   273	
   274	For an optimization, we observe that often many of the values in the
   275	sequence $y_1z_1,\ldots y_nz_n$ are repeated (see
   276	Table~\ref{tab:solutions}). Moreover, because of the specific symmetry
   277	break of Equation~\eqref{eq:symmetry}, repeated values $y_iz_i$ occur
   278	consecutively in this sequence.
   279	%
   280	Instead of encoding the LCM constraints using Equation~\eqref{eq:lcm},
   281	we encode them with the following constraints
   282	\begin{equation}
   283	  \bigwedge_{i=1}^{n-1} \mathtt{if~}(y_iz_i=y_{i+1}z_{i+1})
   284	  \mathtt{~then~} (d_i=d_{i+1}) \mathtt{~else~}(y_iz_i \times d_i = L)
   285	\end{equation}
   286	
   287	In Figure~\ref{modelpart3}, the variables $[\ell_1,\ldots,\ell_n]$ are
   288	such that $y_iz_i\times d_i=\ell_i$. If we constrain all of the $\ell_i$ to
   289	equal $\ell_1$ then $\ell_1$ is a common multiplier of the divisors
   290	($y_i,z_i$). Instead we only constrain $\ell_i=\ell_1$ where the
   291	divisor $y_iz_i$ occurs first (not repeated) in the sequence of
   292	divisors.
   293	
   294	\begin{figure}[t]
   295	  \centering
   296	  \fbox{\[ \begin{array}{l} 
   297	    \displaystyle\bigwedge_{i=1}^{n}
   298	    \set{new\_binary(\ell_i,1,maxL), new\_binary(d_i,1,\lceil maxL/ 11 \rceil), \\
   299	         binary\_times(y_iz_i,d_i,\ell_i)
   300	        } \\
   301	    \displaystyle\bigwedge_{i=1}^{n-1}
   302	    \set{binary\_eq\_reif(y_iz_i,y_{i+1}z_{i+1},a_i),
   303	         binary\_eq\_reif(d_i,d_{i+1},b_i),\\
   304	         binary\_eq\_reif(\ell_{i+1},\ell_1,c_i),
   305	         bool\_array\_or([-a_i,b_i]),
   306	         bool\_array\_or([a_i,c_i])
   307	        }
   308	      \end{array}
   309	      \]}
   310	  \caption{The \bee\ model part 3.}
   311	  \label{modelpart3}
   312	\end{figure}
   313	
   314	
   315	\subsection{The Puzzle Constraint}
   316	\label{section:puzzle_constraints}
   317	
   318	
   319	\begin{figure}[b]
   320	  \centering
   321	  \fbox{\[ \begin{array}{l} 
   322	
   323	    \displaystyle\bigwedge_{i=1}^{n}
   324	    \set{new\_binary(t_i,1,(9/11)\times maxL), binary\_times(x_i,d_i,t_i)
   325	        }\\
   326	    \bigwedge\set{binary\_array\_sum\_eq([t_1,\ldots,t_n],\ell_1)}  \\[7mm]
   327	
   328	  \end{array}\]}
   329	  \caption{The \bee\ model part 4.}
   330	  \label{modelpart4}
   331	\end{figure}
   332	
   333	Equation~\eqref{eq:specification_with_lcm} is
   334	modeled by the following constraint expressed in terms of the
   335	variables $d_i$ introduced in the model as described in
   336	Section~\ref{section:lcm_constraints}.
   337	%
   338	We encode Equation~\eqref{eq:problem_specification} as 
   339	\begin{equation}
   340	 \sum_{i=1}^n x_i\times d_i = L
   341	\end{equation}
   342	For the \bee\ syntax see Figure~\ref{modelpart4}.
   343	
