+
+The paper states no NP-hardness theorem, average-case theorem, or generated
+distribution at all.  Its contribution is a SAT encoding and experimental solution
+of two formerly open fixed values of `n`.  Worst-case hardness cannot be inferred
+from the long `n=36` and `n=39` runs, and there is no theorem-and-parameter regime to
+put honestly in a Track A `hardness_basis`.  Randomly masking a Table 2 answer would
+create a new clue distribution whose difficulty is neither stated nor analyzed in
+the paper; random clues can only make the fixed SAT instance easier.
